metadata:
  node_type: memory
  type: project
  originSessionId: 05f7792c-0c35-44d1-afc1-3cee9ccf61e1
  modified: 2026-10-13T15:12:39.099Z
---

**Renamed 2026-10-14 (branch claude/no-save-prompts, dfd89a1379):** the code is now `skipped_confirmation`, rules in `SkippedConfirmations.hpp` (`skipped_edit_confirmations`, `skipped_save_confirmations` — the latter covers Tab::save_preset's first-layer temperature check). Kenneth found "orca_question" a bad name; use "skipped confirmation" when discussing it.

On 2026-10-12 Kenneth decided how the settings tools treat the questions OrcaSlicer asks only when a person edits a field (`Tab::on_value_change`, which `Tab::load_config` never runs). He chose, in his words, to "forward this text to the agent" and let the agent decide one of three: go ahead silently, go ahead and tell the user, or ask the user as an extra turn. He then picked "way 1": our own copy of OrcaSlicer's conditions, guarded by a test.

**What was built** (branch `claude/edit-time-questions`, worktree `.claude/worktrees/exciting-feistel-6dc0c0`, landed on jusprin-newui as 2f308f2dda by fast-forward and pushed, 2026-10-12): `forward_edit_questions` in `src/slic3r/GUI/JusPrin/Workspace/OrcaEditQuestions.hpp`, called from `OrcaWorkspaceAdapter::preview_settings`. One preview warning per question, code `orca_question`, message = OrcaSlicer's words, `suggestions` = the values its Yes would set. The patch stays valid and applies as sent. The `settings_preview_patch` description tells the agent the three choices. Guide section: "Questions OrcaSlicer asks a person who edits a field" in `agent-docs/jusprin/mcp-tool-extension-guide.md`.

**Why:** a valid tool call already starts at once with no approval step; a "the person agreed" field would only be the agent's word. I had proposed a refuse-until-agreed gate for `make_overhang_printable`; Kenneth's design replaced it.

**How to apply:**
- Do not add a gate, a blacklist entry, or an approval step for these settings. Do not call OrcaSlicer's handler with a dialog listener from product code.
- A question fires only for a key the patch really changes (in `result.changes`, so after the normalizer), with its condition read from the patch's values before normalization. Not for a state that already exists.
- The test is the guard: `verify_edit_questions` and the sweep in `tests/workspace/real_adapter_harness.cpp` set the value in the tab's config, call the real `tab->on_value_change`, capture dialogs with `ScopedDialogListener`, and compare text with what preview forwarded. After an upstream update a mismatch reads `Orca asks ..., preview forwarded ...`.
- My interpretation, not Kenneth's words: messages are looked up with `_u8L` per piece, so the agent gets the interface language.

**Traps found while building:**
- The English catalog (`localization/i18n/en`) rewords some msgids ("smooth timelapse" shows as "smooth timelapse mode ... the prime tower"). What a person sees is the catalog text, not the literal in `Tab.cpp`. Copy the msgid and translate; never hard-code the English.
- `enable_wrapping_detection` is silently turned back off on printers whose type lacks it (X1C); P2S and H2D have it. The harness sets `printer_model` to "Bambu Lab P2S" for those cases.
- `sparse_infill_rotate_template`'s question depends on `m_last_sparse_infill_rotate_template_value`, which `Tab::reload_config` resets; emulate the hand edit before any `load_config`, not after an apply.
- The handler `any_cast`s the field value for a few keys: bool, int for enums, `std::string`, `unsigned char` for per-extruder switches, double. The printer tab's per-extruder keys carry `#0`.

**Still open (told to Kenneth):** things the handler does without asking that a patch skips: flush volume recalculation after a long-retraction switch, `filament_diameter` and `pellet_flow_coefficient` kept in step, `thumbnails_format` from `thumbnails`. How well a model picks among the three choices is unmeasured; that needs a live-model batch he approves ([[prompt-eval-spend-budget]]). See [[settings-write-blacklist]], [[prove-guard-tests-fail-first]].

**Rename 2026-10-14 (commit 336facf825 on jusprin-newui):** the warning code `orca_question` is now `skipped_confirmation`, and the rules moved to `Workspace/SkippedConfirmations.hpp` as `skipped_edit_confirmations` and `skipped_save_confirmations`; the save-time filament temperature question is forwarded the same way. Everything above that says `orca_question` means `skipped_confirmation`.
#pragma once

// Skipped confirmations: the confirmations OrcaSlicer asks of a person who
// changes a setting by hand, or saves a filament, which a settings patch
// skips. A skipped confirmation is not a refusal: the patch is valid and
// applies as sent. Preview hands each one to the caller as a warning with
// code skipped_confirmation, so the caller settles it: go ahead, go ahead and
// tell the user, or ask the user first when the answer is theirs.
//
// Tab::on_value_change (Tab.cpp) asks while a field is edited, one field at a
// time; a patch goes through Tab::load_config, which never runs it. Each
// warning carries the words the person would read as its message and, under
// `suggestions`, the values OrcaSlicer's Yes would set.
//
// The conditions and the message ids are copied from Tab.cpp, which upstream
// changes. Each id is looked up in the catalog as Tab.cpp looks it up, piece
// by piece, because the catalog rewords them, in English too. The workspace
// adapter harness holds the copy to the original: it makes each change the
// way a field does, lets OrcaSlicer's handler run, and compares the dialogs
// it opens with the questions here, for the cases below and for every
// writable setting (verify_skipped_confirmations and verify_every_writable_setting
// in tests/workspace/real_adapter_harness.cpp).

#include "OrcaSettingDefinitions.hpp"
#include "libslic3r/PresetBundle.hpp"
#include "slic3r/GUI/GUI_App.hpp"
#include "slic3r/GUI/I18N.hpp"

#include <algorithm>

namespace Slic3r::GUI::JusPrin::Workspace {

inline constexpr const char* kSkippedConfirmation = "skipped_confirmation";

// `changes` are the patch's settings that take another value; `before` and
// `after` are the scope's whole configuration around the patch as written
// (for an object, the process with the object's overrides). OrcaSlicer asks
// about the field being changed, and reads its condition from the values the
// change leaves, before it normalizes any of them.
inline void skipped_edit_confirmations(SettingsScope scope, const std::vector<SettingChange>& changes, const DynamicPrintConfig& before,
                                       const DynamicPrintConfig& after, std::vector<SettingIssue>& warnings)
{
    const auto changed = [&](const char* key) {
        return std::any_of(changes.begin(), changes.end(), [key](const SettingChange& change) { return change.key == key; });
    };
    // A switch held once per extruder, turned on for one of them.
    const auto turned_on = [&](const char* key) {
        const auto* was = dynamic_cast<const ConfigOptionVector<unsigned char>*>(before.option(key));
        const auto* now = dynamic_cast<const ConfigOptionVector<unsigned char>*>(after.option(key));
        if (!changed(key) || was == nullptr || now == nullptr)
            return false;
        for (std::size_t index = 0; index < now->values.size(); ++index)
            if (now->values[index] == 1 && (index >= was->values.size() || was->values[index] != 1))
                return true;
        return false;
    };
    const auto ask = [&](const char* key, std::string words, std::vector<std::string> yes = {}) {
        warnings.push_back({key, kSkippedConfirmation, std::move(words), {}, std::move(yes)});
    };

    if (scope == SettingsScope::Filament) {
        if (turned_on("filament_long_retractions_when_cut"))
            ask("filament_long_retractions_when_cut",
                _u8L("Experimental feature: Retracting and cutting off the filament at a greater distance during filament changes to minimize flush. "
                     "Although it can notably reduce flush, it may also elevate the risk of nozzle clogs or other printing complications. "
                     "Please use with the latest printer firmware."));
        return;
    }
    if (scope == SettingsScope::Printer) {
        if (turned_on("long_retractions_when_cut"))
            ask("long_retractions_when_cut",
                _u8L("Experimental feature: Retracting and cutting off the filament at a greater distance during filament changes to minimize flush. "
                     "Although it can notably reduce flush, it may also elevate the risk of nozzle clogs or other printing complications."));
        return;
    }

    PresetBundle&             bundle  = *wxGetApp().preset_bundle;
    const DynamicPrintConfig& printer = bundle.printers.get_edited_preset().config;
    const bool                tower   = after.opt_bool("enable_prime_tower");
    const bool                smooth  = after.opt_enum<TimelapseType>("timelapse_type") == tlSmooth;

    bool mcp_bridge{false};
    bool header_visual{false};
    bool right_pane_visual{false};
    bool task_chat_configured{false};
    bool figma_timeline_manual{false};
    std::optional<bool> dark_appearance;
    fs::path capture_dir;
    fs::path file_corpus_manifest;
    fs::path file_corpus_output;
    fs::path file_corpus_config;
    // --plan-journey: the model to load and the directory turns pass through.
    fs::path journey_model;
    fs::path journey_dir;
    fs::path prompt_script;
    fs::path prompt_requests;
    // --request-log: where the project chat's requests are written, or empty.
    fs::path request_log;
    // --printer-connect-capture: the connect flow only, pictured.
    bool connect_capture{false};
    // --printer-live-no-plugin: connecting a Bambu Lab printer without the
    // network plug-in, and without the fake printer standing in for it.
    bool no_plugin{false};
    // --fake-printer: the in-process fake Bambu printer is on, and found
    // on the network, in a hands-on run (Testing/README.md); with
    // --manual-mcp, the fixture's saved printer is connected to it.
    bool fake_printer{false};
    // --printer-settings-live, --filament-settings-live: a settings change to
    // the printer, or slot 1's filament, OrcaSlicer ships, from the header.
    bool settings_live{false};
    bool filament_settings_live{false};
    bool keep_data{false};
    std::shared_ptr<JusPrinTest::StdioClient> bridge;
};

